      "<strong>v1.28.0 &mdash; Karen real-tenant rename: dormantspaowner@test.com &rarr; karen.rejuv@gmail.com.</strong> The v1.27 ship arc (shipped + dry-run-verified earlier 2026-05-30, see session_story_2026-05-30_v127_keystone_shipped) proved the waitlist-invite code end-to-end on the dormantspaowner tenant &mdash; but dormantspaowner was the &lsquo;admin&rsquo;s data bucket&rsquo; stand-in, not Karen-as-herself. v1.28.0 closes that gap: rename the auth identity in place so Karen logs in with her real email and inherits the entire data scope (1,140 patients + 5,754 appointments + the v1.27 emma_noshow_policies seed + the 5 emma_waitlist rows from tonight&rsquo;s dry-run) with zero foreign-key churn. <strong>Why rename-in-place vs new-identity-plus-migration</strong>: same user_id (<code>ecf8bcee-24a6-4a4d-a5f4-381166ca57fc</code>) means no UPDATE sweep across the ~15 user-scoped tables (knowledge_nodes, emma_appointments, emma_waitlist, emma_waitlist_tokens, emma_noshow_policies, emma_reliability_status, emma_recovery_events, emma_rescue_attempts/offers, patient_outreach/state, user_prefs, etc.). One line at the auth layer + one line at the display layer, atomic in a single transaction. The alternative would have generated 15&times; more SQL surface for the same end-state, with real risk of orphaning data on a missed table. <strong>What shipped (DB-side, paste into Supabase dashboard)</strong>: <code>supabase/migrations/20260601000000_v128_0_karen_real_tenant_rename.sql</code>. Single transaction: <code>UPDATE auth.users SET email='karen.rejuv@gmail.com', email_confirmed_at=COALESCE(email_confirmed_at, NOW())</code> on the dormantspaowner user_id (the COALESCE keeps magic-link-ready in case the prior email_confirmed_at was NULL); <code>UPDATE public.tenants SET name='Rejuv Skin Spa'</code> on the tenant_id joined through tenant_memberships where role='owner' (drops the &lsquo;[Dormant]&rsquo; tag from v1.26.21). <strong>Side effect</strong>: admin@refill.platform&rsquo;s default-fallback landing tenant&rsquo;s display name flips from &lsquo;[Dormant] Rejuv Skin Spa&rsquo; to &lsquo;Rejuv Skin Spa&rsquo; — same user_id, same data, just Karen-the-real-user owns it now. Admin still sees the bucket when defaulted-landing. <strong>What this DOESN&rsquo;T change</strong>: PERSONA_EMAILS.karen in <code>src/lib/personas.ts</code> still routes the public /personas magic-link mint flow to <code>testspaowner@test.com</code> (the *.test TLD demo tenant, per the v417 safety rail in <code>src/server/v417-persona-mint.ts</code>); Karen&rsquo;s real production tenant is OUTSIDE the public mint flow by design. <code>rescue_proxy_email</code> on Karen&rsquo;s tenant stays at <code>davidfand303@gmail.com</code> for now (Grasshopper&rsquo;s Mac is the iMessage MCP draft processor — concierge-first per <code>project_concierge_first_activation</code>); flipping it to Karen&rsquo;s own email so blue-bubbles originate from her actual Apple ID is the v1.28.1 follow-up once her Mac side is wired with Claude Desktop + imessage-draft MCP. <strong>First-login path for Karen</strong>: magic-link via Supabase dashboard Auth panel (Authentication &rarr; Users &rarr; karen.rejuv@gmail.com &rarr; Send Magic Link). She taps the link in Gmail, the v410.2 cross-host bridge lands her pre-authenticated on app.getrefill.app. No password setup needed. <strong>What this DOESN&rsquo;T fix</strong>: any browser session previously logged in as dormantspaowner@test.com (none expected — admin uses admin@refill.platform) would invalidate on the email rename per Supabase&rsquo;s refresh-token cache behavior. The 4 paused emma_waitlist rows from tonight&rsquo;s dry-run (Stein, Moench, Utter, Yelenick) remain attached to Karen&rsquo;s now-real tenant &mdash; harmless paused state, defer to single pre-launch sweep per <code>feedback_test_data_cleanup_deferred</code>. <strong>Touched</strong>: <code>supabase/migrations/20260601000000_v128_0_karen_real_tenant_rename.sql</code> (new file, the migration), <code>src/lib/changelog.ts</code> (this entry). No worker-code change &mdash; pure data ship.",
