      "<strong>v1.26.19 &mdash; Fold the three near-duplicate spa-profile readers into a single shared module.</strong> v1.26.18 closed the <code>phone_numbers</code> schema-vs-code gap by pointing <code>resolveSpaFromNumber</code> at <code>knowledge_nodes</code>, but left the three near-duplicate implementations (one each in <code>emma-rescue.functions.ts</code>, <code>emma-preshow.functions.ts</code>, <code>emma-blast.functions.ts</code>) copy-pasted across the surface area. <code>resolveSpaName</code> was 2-way duplicated (rescue + preshow) on the same shape, and <code>resolveOwnerDisplayName</code> lived solo in rescue. The v1.26.18 entry called this folding out as the &lsquo;future DRY pass&rsquo; &mdash; this is that pass. <strong>What shipped</strong>: new <code>src/server/emma-spa-profile.ts</code> exports <code>resolveSpaName</code>, <code>resolveSpaFromNumber</code>, and <code>resolveOwnerDisplayName</code> as the canonical readers for the <code>knowledge_nodes</code> spa-profile bucket. The three Emma surfaces import from <code>@/server/emma-spa-profile</code> and drop their local copies. Signatures are unchanged at call sites; bodies are bit-for-bit ports. <strong>Why <code>src/server</code> not <code>src/lib</code></strong>: only server functions (cron sweeps, dispatcher fns, scheduler) call these readers; none are reachable from the client bundle. <code>src/lib/personas.ts</code> belongs in lib because <code>PersonaSwitcher.tsx</code> is client code; this helper has no client consumer and therefore lives next to the Emma server fns that use it. <strong>Type shape</strong>: the helper accepts <code>SupabaseClient&lt;Database&gt;</code> (the structural type each surface&rsquo;s local <code>admin()</code> returns), so existing call sites compile unchanged without dragging each file&rsquo;s local <code>SupabaseAdmin</code> alias into a shared definition. <strong>Why <code>resolveOwnerDisplayName</code> moves too (despite being single-source)</strong>: it reads the same <code>spa-profile</code> bucket with the same shape; keeping it inline in <code>emma-rescue.functions.ts</code> while extracting the other two would leave a footgun for the next surface (e.g. preshow gaining a personalized greeting) to re-duplicate. All four spa-profile lookup-key readers (spa-name, from-number, owner-display-name, plus future additions like hours / address / website) now have one canonical home. <strong>Net effect</strong>: next spa-profile <code>lookup_key</code> is one new exported fn in one file, picked up by all three surfaces. The Settings UI for editing these scalars (v1.27 candidate) gets a single import target. <strong>What this DOESN&rsquo;T fix</strong>: the three surfaces each still define their own local <code>admin()</code> helper that mints a service-role <code>SupabaseClient&lt;Database&gt;</code> from the same env vars &mdash; that&rsquo;s a separate copy-paste pattern outside the spa-profile-reader scope and out of scope for this ship. Same for the per-file <code>SupabaseAdmin</code> type aliases (each is <code>ReturnType&lt;typeof admin&gt;</code>; identical resolution, separate declarations). <strong>Touched</strong>: <code>src/server/emma-spa-profile.ts</code> (new file, canonical home), <code>src/server/emma-rescue.functions.ts</code> (import + delete 3 local fns), <code>src/server/emma-preshow.functions.ts</code> (import + delete 2 local fns), <code>src/server/emma-blast.functions.ts</code> (import + delete 1 local fn + docblock), <code>src/lib/changelog.ts</code> (this entry). No migration. No behavior change.",
