      "<strong>v1.26.9 &mdash; Tier-recompute observability + bulk-mode refactor of the cron sweep path.</strong> Two interlocking fixes triggered by the &lsquo;Anderson David has 11 cancels but tier=trusted&rsquo; observation from the v1.26.4 verification screenshot. <strong>Root cause</strong>: the v1.25.7 RPC fixed the COUNTS-side of the bulk-ingest staleness gap (no_shows_6mo, cancellations_6mo, total_visits, plus v1.26.4&rsquo;s lifetime variants) but explicitly left tier computation in TS &mdash; <em>which means the same per-patient round-trip loop that v1.25.7 was created to eliminate still ran on the tier path, just on a different cadence</em>. The daily cron at 02:00 UTC POSTs to <code>/api/cron/emma-reliability-sweep</code> which iterates every spa and calls <code>recomputeReliabilityForUser</code>. Pre-v1.26.9 that fn looped <code>recomputeReliabilityForPatient</code> per patient: ~4-6 DB round trips each. At Karen-scale (~700 patients with appointment history) that&rsquo;s 2800-4200 RTs &mdash; well past Cloudflare Workers&rsquo; 30s CPU timeout. Cron was almost certainly timing out silently after Karen&rsquo;s 5,754-row Acuity ingest, leaving tiers indefinitely stale. Anderson&rsquo;s &lsquo;trusted&rsquo; tier with 11 cancels was the canary. <strong>Bulk refactor of recomputeReliabilityForUser</strong>: step 1 calls the v1.25.7 RPC for fresh counts (single SQL), step 2 reads policy thresholds (1 RT), step 3 reads ALL reliability rows back (1 RT), step 4 computes new tier per row IN MEMORY via the existing TS <code>computeTier</code> (no DB round trip), step 5 bulk-writes tier changes via chunked Promise.all&rsquo;d UPDATEs (chunk=50, ~1 RT per 50 changes), step 6 batch-inserts pattern_alerts for meaningful transitions (1 RT for patient names + 1 RT for the alerts insert). Total: ~5-15 RTs regardless of patient count. The per-patient TS path (<code>recomputeReliabilityForPatient</code>) is unchanged &mdash; single-patient calls from <code>updateAppointmentStatus</code> have no scale issue. Both paths coexist. The cron route now gets the bulk benefit transparently because it calls the refactored fn. <strong>New observability surface on the A-list rules page</strong>: a &lsquo;Reliability data&rsquo; section at the bottom of the page shows <code>Last computed: X &middot; N patients tracked</code> sourced from <code>max(recomputed_at)</code> across the tenant&rsquo;s reliability rows. Adjacent &lsquo;Recompute now&rsquo; button forces an immediate sweep via the new <code>recomputeReliabilityNow</code> server fn. Toast surfaces <code>X patients recomputed &middot; N tier transitions</code>. Reloads the page data so flagged-count picks up newly in_recovery patients. <strong>New server fns</strong>: <code>getReliabilityFreshness({accessToken, viewAsUserId?}) =&gt; { latestRecomputedAt, patientsTracked }</code> &mdash; parallel max/count queries, single round trip aggregate. <code>recomputeReliabilityNow({accessToken, viewAsUserId?}) =&gt; { patientsRecomputed, transitions, completedAt }</code> &mdash; resolveEffectiveUserId &rarr; recomputeReliabilityForUser (which is now bulk). Both impersonation-aware per the v1.20+ pattern. <strong>What Grasshopper should expect on click</strong>: with 5,754 appointments and 695 matched patients, Anderson David&rsquo;s row should transition from trusted to in_recovery (if noShows6mo &ge; 3) or to regular/vip (if total_visits crosses thresholds). The recap mentioned 11 lifetime cancels &mdash; cancels don&rsquo;t drive tier (no-shows do), so the transition depends on the no_shows_6mo count specifically. Either way: pattern_alerts will fire for any transitions; visible on /app/refill/rescue Patterns tab. The Anderson alert will land too &mdash; that&rsquo;s the system working as designed on real data; not the case for a suppression engine (per the v1.26.7 self/family suppression kill, Anderson is just a real patient record). <strong>Audit-honest note</strong>: <code>composeAlert</code> uses <code>noShows6mo + totalVisits</code> from the in-memory snapshot taken at sweep time, so the alert headline (&lsquo;Pattern: X has N no-shows in the last 6 months&rsquo;) reflects the same numbers that drove the transition decision. No drift between transition condition + alert text. <strong>Pattern lesson banked</strong>: when a denormalization or RPC ship leaves <em>part</em> of the materialized state behind on the TS side (e.g. counts in RPC, tier still in TS), the slow path can stay broken indefinitely under workers timeouts without surfacing &mdash; the cron just silently fails. Future RPC ships should either complete the picture (all dependent columns) or expose the partial-success in observability. Touched: <code>src/server/emma-reliability.functions.ts</code> (recomputeReliabilityForUser bulk refactor + getReliabilityFreshness + recomputeReliabilityNow server fns + types), <code>src/routes/app.refill.patients.a-list-rules.tsx</code> (freshness state + handleRecompute + Reliability data section UI), <code>src/lib/changelog.ts</code> (this entry). No migration &mdash; the v1.25.7 RPC is reused as-is.",
