      "<strong>v1.26.5 &mdash; Hotfix: v1.26.4 silently changed rule behavior. Naming the wrong claim.</strong> v1.26.4 changelog said &lsquo;Rule behavior unchanged &mdash; still filters on 6mo.&rsquo; <strong>That was wrong.</strong> Widening <code>listReliabilityFlags</code>&rsquo; server WHERE to <code>or(no_shows_6mo.gt.0, cancellations_6mo.gt.0, no_shows_lifetime.gt.0, cancellations_lifetime.gt.0)</code> &mdash; necessary to compute lifetime totals for display &mdash; also widened the result set that the client collapsed into <code>unreliableIds</code> via <code>new Set(flags.map(f =&gt; f.patientNodeId))</code>. Because <code>matchesRules</code> filters with <code>unreliableIds.has(p.id)</code>, the rule silently flipped from 6mo-strict (production semantics since v1.25.4) to lifetime-inclusive. On Karen&rsquo;s data the flagged count jumped from 115 to 358 and the &lsquo;Would be added&rsquo; preview shifted accordingly. Verification screenshot caught it. <strong>Also broken</strong>: toggle copy read &lsquo;(last 6 months &middot; 358 flagged &middot; 172 cancels / 2 no-shows)&rsquo; &mdash; mathematically impossible (358 patients can&rsquo;t have 174 events between them in 6mo) because 358 was the lifetime-inclusive union, not a 6mo number. Per the math-must-be-exact rule, the parenthesized count and the leading window-label must agree. <strong>Fix</strong>: client now filters the reliabilityFlags array down to 6mo-positive patients BEFORE building <code>unreliableIds</code>. Lifetime totals stay informational on the second description line, where they belong. Added a small &lsquo;X more patients with older history outside the 6mo window (not excluded by this rule)&rsquo; clause to the second line when the lifetime-only delta is non-zero &mdash; Karen sees the full picture without the rule silently changing under her. <strong>Karen&rsquo;s original &lsquo;never&rsquo; ask remains real</strong> &mdash; switching the filter from 6mo to lifetime (or adding a window selector) is a conscious UX decision for a separate ship, not a side effect of a data-availability ship. <strong>Pattern lesson</strong>: when a data ship widens a server query, the client read-path needs explicit re-validation against rule semantics. The minute I saw &lsquo;the WHERE got an OR with lifetime,&rsquo; I should have audited every consumer of the response, not just the display path. Caching that pattern for future denormalization ships. Touched: <code>src/routes/app.refill.patients.a-list-rules.tsx</code> (unreliableIds memo filters 6mo-positive only + new lifetimeOnlyCount memo + toggle description second-line addendum), <code>src/lib/changelog.ts</code> (this entry).",
