      "<strong>v1.26.4 &mdash; Lifetime cancellation / no-show tracking on emma_reliability_status.</strong> Karen&rsquo;s original A-list ask was &lsquo;exclude anyone who&rsquo;s ever cancelled or no-showed&rsquo; &mdash; but v1.25.4 shipped on the 6mo rolling columns (<code>no_shows_6mo</code> + <code>cancellations_6mo</code>) because that&rsquo;s what was materialized. 6mo is correct for tier semantics (the in_recovery tier must be recoverable when the window rolls past; otherwise patients are unforgivable forever and the reliability engine becomes a one-way trap). But the A-list filter is a different decision &mdash; a spa owner who wants &lsquo;never cancelled&rsquo; as a VIP criterion should be able to see lifetime totals. Two new columns added: <code>no_shows_lifetime</code> + <code>cancellations_lifetime</code>, both <code>integer not null default 0</code>. Migration <code>20260528020000_emma_reliability_lifetime_counts.sql</code> adds the columns, backfills from <code>emma_appointments</code> via a single CTE (same shape as the v1.25.7 RPC body, just no 6mo window filter), and updates the <code>refill_recompute_reliability_counts</code> RPC to populate both lifetime + 6mo in one statement going forward. The per-row TS trigger (<code>recomputeReliabilityForPatient</code> in <code>emma-reliability.functions.ts</code>) also updated to materialize both at appointment-status transitions. <strong>Surface change</strong>: A-list rules toggle description now shows a second line &mdash; &lsquo;Lifetime across all patients: X cancels / Y no-shows&rsquo; &mdash; alongside the existing 6mo line. Singular/plural agreement applied. Karen now sees both numbers and can see how much of the cancellation history is recent vs. accumulated. <strong>Behavior unchanged</strong>: the rule (<code>excludeUnreliable</code>) still filters on the 6mo window today. Switching the filter to lifetime (or adding a window selector) is a separate UX decision &mdash; data first, behavior change as a follow-up if Karen asks. <strong>Pattern note</strong>: tier (good/regular/vip/in_recovery) is NOT lifetime-driven and not changing &mdash; that logic is unchanged, still keyed off <code>in_recovery_threshold</code> + <code>in_recovery_window_months</code> per the spa-configurable policy. <strong>Operator step</strong>: paste <code>supabase/migrations/20260528020000_emma_reliability_lifetime_counts.sql</code> into the Supabase SQL editor before the worker is exercised against the new columns (link in the next message). The TS side gracefully renders &lsquo;0 lifetime&rsquo; until the migration lands &mdash; no crash, just a temporary undercount. Touched: <code>supabase/migrations/20260528020000_emma_reliability_lifetime_counts.sql</code> (new), <code>src/server/emma-reliability.functions.ts</code> (per-row trigger + listReliabilityFlags + ReliabilityFlag type + comment), <code>src/integrations/supabase/types.ts</code> (Row/Insert/Update extended), <code>src/routes/app.refill.patients.a-list-rules.tsx</code> (reliabilityTotals memo extended + toggle description second line), <code>src/lib/changelog.ts</code> (this entry).",
